{"step_index":2435,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-27T10:38:25Z","input_tokens":3110,"cache_read_tokens":155066,"output_tokens":3965,"content":"### Phase 5: Problem Learning Workspace — Complete Implementation & Verification Report\n\nThe **Problem Learning Workspace** has been implemented, integrated with the existing execution visualizer, and verified with **101/101 automated tests passing** (and all 387 tests across all 6 test suites passing with zero regressions).\n\n---\n\n### Architectural Overview of the Problem Learning Workspace\n\nThe workspace provides a dedicated, full-featured educational environment accessible via direct route (e.g., `#dsa/problem/:id`), by clicking any problem title in the Problem Library table, or via the `🎓 Learning Workspace` subnav button.\n\nIt is structured in a **responsive 2-column split layout**:\n\n```\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│  ← Back to Problem Library  /  LeetCode #1: Two Sum  [#dsa/problem/1]  [☆ Bookmark] [🔖 Review] [✅ Solved] [🔗 Copy Route] │\n├───────────────────────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│  LEFT COLUMN: Concept & Pattern Comprehension      │  RIGHT COLUMN: Interactive Practice & Step-Through  │\n│  [📖 A. Understand the Problem] [🧩 B. Learn Pattern] │  [💻 C. Try It Yourself]  [🚀 D. Visualize Execution]│\n├───────────────────────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│  🎯 What the Problem Asks                         │  [▶ Run Code] [🧪 Run Tests] [↺ Reset] [💡 Hints]   │\n│  📤 What You Need to Calculate or Return (Goal)    │  🐍 Python 3 (Restricted Safe Offline Engine)      │\n│  📋 Input & Output Examples (with Explanations)    │  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │\n│  📏 Important Constraints                         │  │ def twoSum(nums, target):                     │ │\n│  ⚠️ Common Edge Cases to Consider                 │  │     ...                                       │ │\n│  🔒 Reference Solution Hidden Banner              │  └───────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │\n│                                                   │  🧪 Test Results Panel (Passed / Failed Cards)     │\n│  (Or in Tab B: Relevant DSA Pattern, Why Useful   │  💻 Terminal / Output Console (Stdout & Variables) │\n│   Naive vs Optimal, Prerequisites, Walkthrough,   │  💡 Progressive Hints (Hint 1 → 2 → 3)             │\n│   and 3-Step Intended Approach)                   │  🔓 Reference Solution Card (with Confirmation)    │\n└───────────────────────────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n### Key Capabilities Delivered\n\n#### A. Understand the Problem (Zero AI APIs — 100% Rule-Based)\n- **What the Problem Asks**: Clear, plain-English breakdown of the problem's requirements.\n- **Return Goal Callout**: Explicitly tells the learner whether they need to return indices, boolean flags, numeric optimal values, or arrays.\n- **Input & Output Examples**: Formatted cards showing input values, expected outputs, and step-by-step explanations.\n- **Important Constraints & Bounds**: Array lengths, value limits, and memory/time constraints.\n- **Common Edge Cases**: Explicitly highlights empty/single-element inputs, duplicate elements, negative numbers, and boundary conditions.\n- **Solution Hidden by Default**: A clear lock banner encourages independent problem-solving before peeking.\n\n#### B. Learn the Pattern (DSA Teaching Content)\n- **Relevant Pattern Identification**: Displays the primary pattern (e.g., *Hash Map Lookup*, *Two Pointers*, *Sliding Window*, *Monotonic Stack*, *Dynamic Programming*).\n- **Why This Pattern is Useful**: Contrasts naive brute-force complexity (e.g., $O(n^2)$) with the optimal complexity (e.g., $O(n)$) achieved by the pattern.\n- **Prerequisite Concepts**: Interactive concept tags linking required foundational techniques.\n- **Illustrative Walkthrough Example**: Traces how state invariants mutate on a small 2-to-3 element sample.\n- **3-Step Intended Approach**: Clear, actionable 1-2-3 guide (*Initialize State $\\rightarrow$ Iterate & Update Invariant $\\rightarrow$ Finalize Result*).\n\n#### C. Try It Yourself (Safe Client-Side Execution & Testing)\n- **Interactive Python Editor**: Code editor pre-populated with starter code or restored from user's local edits.\n- **Local Persistence & Auto-Save**: Auto-saves keystrokes to browser `localStorage` under `dsa_user_code_v1`.\n- **Safe Offline Code Execution (`runWorkspaceCode`)**: Executes entirely client-side using `window.PythonExecutionEngine.trace()`. Never sends user code to any server; handles syntax and runtime errors safely without app crashes.\n- **Automated Multi-Case Test Runner (`runWorkspaceTests`)**: Compares actual outputs with expected test case outputs and generates color-coded pass/fail cards. Automatically marks the problem as `Solved` upon 100% test pass.\n- **Progressive Hints (`revealedHints`)**: Progressive hints (Hint 1: Key observation, Hint 2: Auxiliary data structure, Hint 3: Algorithmic invariant) revealed one at a time.\n- **Reference Solution Reveal with Confirmation**: Requires user confirmation before revealing the optimal reference code, accompanied by verified complexity chips (`Time: O(n)`, `Space: O(n)`) and a `📋 Copy to Editor` button.\n- **Reset Code**: Restores the original starter template in a single click.\n\n#### D. Visualize Execution Integration\n- **Direct Integration with Code Flow Visualizer**: `loadAndVisualizeProblem(id, codeToRun)` feeds the learner's customized or reference code directly into `window.teachMeApp`.\n- **Full Preservation of Core Features**: Active executing line highlighting, variable boxes, arithmetic animation stages, condition comparison gates, loop iteration dry-run groups, prediction quiz mode, and bidirectional navig